# Pitfall semantic checks: embeddings from an endpoint, not PyTorch

## Context

Four of the nineteen ontology-pitfall checks — P4.2 (synonyms in superclasses),
P4.4 (subclasses with the same semantics as their superclass), P4.5 (synonyms in
properties) and the P10 candidate pass — compare label and comment *meaning* by
cosine similarity. Upstream did that with `sentence-transformers` in-process,
which brings PyTorch: the image goes from 0.50 GB to **6.02 GB** (measured), and
at that size cold starts and rollouts stall on `PullingImage`. So the extra was
opt-in, and in practice the semantic checks never ran in a deployment.

Embedding a few hundred short strings is a service call.

## Changes

1. `src/back/core/external/pitfalls/embedders.py` (new) — the seam is one method,
   `encode(texts) -> ndarray`. `EndpointEmbedder` posts to the OpenAI-compatible
   `/embeddings` path on the provider already configured for chat (batched at 96,
   MLflow-traced, ordered by the response `index` because providers do not
   guarantee order, empty strings kept as zero-vector slots rather than sent).
   `LocalEmbedder` keeps the original path for air-gapped installs. Checks,
   thresholds and the 0.4/0.6 label/description weighting are untouched.
2. `cosine_similarity` is now four lines of numpy in that module —
   scikit-learn and scipy were in the dependency set for that single function,
   and numpy is already present in the base install via pandas. Zero vectors get
   a 1e-12 norm floor so an absent `rdfs:comment` yields 0 similarity instead of
   poisoning a matrix with `nan`.
3. `src/shared/config/LLMTarget.py` — `embeddings_url()`, `embedding_model()` and
   `for_embeddings()`, sharing `base_url`/`api_key` with chat because an operator
   configures one provider. `ONTOBRICKS_EMBEDDING_MODEL` is separate from
   `ONTOBRICKS_LLM_MODEL` because a chat model cannot serve `/embeddings`, and
   guessing a name yields a provider 404 that reads as a broken endpoint.
4. `src/back/core/external/pitfalls/runner.py` — six `encode` call sites moved to
   the seam; no `sentence_transformers` or `sklearn` import remains, so importing
   the runner no longer pulls PyTorch (verified).
5. **`_DEPS_AVAILABLE` was over-gating.** It required the whole torch stack for
   *all nineteen* checks, so the fifteen that never touch embeddings were
   unavailable without it too. It now means "numpy present", and each family
   raises for itself: the WordNet checks name the `pitfalls` extra, the semantic
   checks name either the endpoint variable or the local extra.

## The eval, and what it found

`tests/eval/run_pitfalls_embedders.py` (new). The thresholds are absolute
constants (`threshold: float = 0.8`) tuned for `all-MiniLM-L6-v2`, so a different
model can flag a different set of pairs with an identical algorithm. Measured
against the three embedding endpoints in `classic-steve-eastus`, on a labelled
probe set of synonym vs unrelated ontology labels:

| model | margin | min synonym | max unrelated | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `databricks-gte-large-en` | **0.333** | 0.807 | 0.593 | separable — **recommended** |
| `databricks-qwen3-embedding-0-6b` | 0.298 | 0.616 | 0.591 | separable, thin gap |
| `databricks-bge-large-en` | 0.166 | 0.644 | 0.679 | **overlaps — unusable** |

Full comparison against the incumbent at the unchanged 0.8 threshold:

| | precision | recall | F1 | missed synonyms |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `gte-large-en` (endpoint) | 1.00 | **1.00** | **1.00** | 0 |
| `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` (in-process) | 1.00 | 0.33 | 0.50 | **4 of 6** |

So the endpoint path is not merely lighter — with `gte-large-en` it is *more
accurate than the model it replaces*, which is why the thresholds needed no
retuning. `bge-large-en` would silently report almost nothing: its synonym mean
(0.77) sits below the 0.8 cut. Aggregate **0.9594**.

### A correction to my own harness

The first version scored `agreement` — Jaccard overlap with the incumbent — at
weight 0.30, and printed **"DO NOT SWAP: models disagree about meaning"**. That
was wrong. The endpoint flagged all six known synonyms, the incumbent two, and
`only_local` was empty: a strict superset, correct on every labelled pair. Scoring
against the incumbent treats it as ground truth when it is the thing being
replaced. Since the probe pairs are labelled, accuracy is now measured against the
labels (`labelled_accuracy`, weight 0.35) and incumbent overlap is reported as
context. The verdict logic also considers direction, so "more sensitive with no
false positives" reads as an improvement rather than a disagreement.

## Deliberately not done

The heavy members belong in a separate `pitfalls-local` extra, leaving `pitfalls`
as nltk-only. I implemented that and reverted it: with `--frozen`, uv reads extras
from `uv.lock`, not `pyproject.toml`, so a new extra name needs a re-lock — and a
full re-resolve fails outright, because torch 2.14 depends on a
`cuda-toolkit[cublas]==13.0.3` that does not exist, for the Python >= 3.15 end of
`requires-python`. Verified pre-existing against an untouched baseline: the
committed lock predates those releases. Not worth risking `uv.lock` (which I
poisoned with proxy URLs once already this release) for a cosmetic rename,
especially as the goal is met regardless — the endpoint path needs no extra at
all. Documented in `pyproject.toml` as one job.

